### What is the problem with a naive @Scheduled job on a large dataset?

A naive scheduled job calls `repository.findAll()`, which loads every row into a `List` and every entity into the persistence context with a dirty-checking snapshot. For millions of rows this exhausts the heap and causes `OutOfMemoryError`. Even if memory holds, a single-threaded loop over millions of rows can run for hours, overlap the next trigger, and starve the database.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.util.List;

@Component
public class NaiveInterestJob {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public NaiveInterestJob(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
    public void accrueInterest() {
        // WRONG: loads every row and snapshot into the heap
        List<Account> all = accountRepo.findAll();
        for (Account a : all) {
            a.accrueInterest(); // dirty checking queues UPDATE
        }
        // flush at commit -- but memory already exploded
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output for 10 million accounts:
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
```

### How do you fix the memory problem with chunked processing?

The fix is keyset pagination: fetch a bounded page, process it, flush and clear, then fetch the next page using the last id seen. After each chunk, `flush()` pushes SQL to the database and `clear()` detaches all entities so the garbage collector reclaims them.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.PageRequest;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Component
public class ChunkedInterestJob {

    private static final int CHUNK = 500;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final EntityManager em;

    public ChunkedInterestJob(AccountRepository accountRepo, EntityManager em) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
    public void accrueInterest() {
        String cursor = "";
        Page<Account> page;
        do {
            page = accountRepo.findByIdGreaterThan(cursor, PageRequest.of(0, CHUNK));
            for (Account a : page.getContent()) {
                a.accrueInterest();
            }
            em.flush();    // push this chunk's UPDATEs to the database
            em.clear();    // detach all entities -- free memory
            if (!page.isEmpty()) {
                cursor = page.getContent().get(page.getContent().size() - 1).getId();
            }
        } while (!page.isEmpty());
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- Memory stays flat in a sawtooth pattern (rises during chunk, drops after clear)
- 10 million accounts processed in 20,000 chunks of 500
```

### How do you handle long execution and prevent overlapping runs?

A single run over millions of rows can last hours and overlap the next trigger. By default Spring's single-threaded scheduler will not start the next run until the current finishes, but if you use a pool you must guard with a lock. Bound the work per run and commit per chunk so progress is durable.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import net.javacrumbs.shedlock.spring.annotation.SchedulerLock;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class GuardedInterestJob {

    @Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
    @SchedulerLock(name = "nightlyInterest",
                   lockAtMostFor = "PT2H",
                   lockAtLeastFor = "PT5M")
    public void accrueInterest() {
        // Only one instance runs; lock auto-releases after 2 hours if crashed
        interestService.processAllChunks();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- Instance A acquires the lock and runs the job
- Instance B finds the lock held and skips
- If Instance A crashes, lock auto-releases after 2 hours
```

### How do you throttle database load?

Unbounded queries and tight loops saturate the database and starve live banking traffic. Use bounded chunk reads, a sensible `fetchSize`, batched writes with `jdbc.batch_size`, and a small pause between chunks.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class ThrottledBatchService {

    private static final int CHUNK = 500;
    private static final long THROTTLE_MS = 100;
    private final EntityManager em;

    public ThrottledBatchService(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    public void processAllChunks() {
        String cursor = "";
        List<Object[]> ids;
        do {
            ids = processOneChunk(cursor);
            if (!ids.isEmpty()) {
                cursor = (String) ids.get(ids.size() - 1)[0];
            }
            try { Thread.sleep(THROTTLE_MS); } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                break;
            }
        } while (!ids.isEmpty());
    }

    @Transactional
    public List<Object[]> processOneChunk(String cursor) {
        // Each chunk is its own transaction
        // Use bounded reads, batched writes, and a fetch size
        return List.of(); // placeholder
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- 100ms pause between chunks gives the database breathing room
- Live banking traffic is not starved during the batch window
```

### How do you handle errors without losing progress?

If the job runs as one giant transaction and fails at row nine million, everything rolls back and the night is wasted. Use per-chunk transactions so completed chunks stay committed, and skip-and-log poison records.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.util.List;

@Service
public class ResilientChunkProcessor {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ResilientChunkProcessor.class);
    private final EntityManager em;

    public ResilientChunkProcessor(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void processChunk(List<Account> chunk) {
        for (Account a : chunk) {
            try {
                a.accrueInterest();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                log.warn("Skipping account {}: {}", a.getId(), e.getMessage());
                // Record the failure for later review
            }
        }
        em.flush();
        em.clear();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WARN  Skipping account SAV8821: Division by zero in rate calculation
-- Job continues with remaining accounts --
```

### How do you make the job resumable with a checkpoint?

Record the last processed cursor after each chunk commit. On restart, read the checkpoint and resume from that point. This makes the job idempotent and resumable.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import com.bank.model.JobCheckpoint;
import com.bank.repository.JobCheckpointRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class CheckpointService {

    private final JobCheckpointRepository checkpointRepo;

    public CheckpointService(JobCheckpointRepository checkpointRepo) {
        this.checkpointRepo = checkpointRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void saveCheckpoint(String jobName, String lastCursor) {
        JobCheckpoint cp = checkpointRepo.findByJobName(jobName)
                .orElse(new JobCheckpoint(jobName));
        cp.setLastCursor(lastCursor);
        checkpointRepo.save(cp);
    }

    public String loadCheckpoint(String jobName) {
        return checkpointRepo.findByJobName(jobName)
                .map(JobCheckpoint::getLastCursor)
                .orElse("");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- Job crashes after processing 500,000 accounts
- Checkpoint table has lastCursor = 'SAV500000'
- Next run resumes from SAV500000, not from the beginning
```

### When should you use Spring Batch instead of a hand-written loop?

For genuinely large, restartable, auditable batch work, Spring Batch is the production-grade answer. It provides chunk-oriented processing, a `JobRepository` that persists progress, restartability, skip/retry policies, and metrics out of the box.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.batch.core.Job;
import org.springframework.batch.core.Step;
import org.springframework.batch.core.job.builder.JobBuilder;
import org.springframework.batch.core.repository.JobRepository;
import org.springframework.batch.core.step.builder.StepBuilder;
import org.springframework.batch.item.database.JpaPagingItemReader;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;

@Configuration
public class InterestBatchConfig {

    @Bean
    public Job interestJob(JobRepository jobRepository, Step interestStep) {
        return new JobBuilder("nightlyInterest", jobRepository)
                .start(interestStep)
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public Step interestStep(JobRepository jobRepository,
                             PlatformTransactionManager txManager) {
        return new StepBuilder("interestStep", jobRepository)
                .<Account, Account>chunk(500, txManager)
                .reader(accountReader())
                .processor(account -> { account.accrueInterest(); return account; })
                .writer(chunk -> { /* dirty checking handles UPDATEs */ })
                .faultTolerant()
                .skip(Exception.class).skipLimit(100)
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public JpaPagingItemReader<Account> accountReader() {
        JpaPagingItemReader<Account> reader = new JpaPagingItemReader<>();
        reader.setQueryString("SELECT a FROM Account a ORDER BY a.id");
        reader.setPageSize(500);
        return reader;
    }
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
- Spring Batch processes 500 accounts per chunk
- Progress persisted in BATCH_STEP_EXECUTION table
- On crash, restart resumes from the last committed chunk
- Up to 100 bad records skipped without aborting
```

### What are common mistakes when processing large datasets in scheduled jobs?

The most common mistake is calling `findAll()` inside a scheduled job. The second is running the entire job in one transaction so a late failure rolls back everything.

```java
// WRONG: one giant transaction, no chunking, no error handling
@Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
@Transactional
public void naiveJob() {
    List<Account> all = accountRepo.findAll(); // OOM
    for (Account a : all) {
        a.accrueInterest(); // one failure rolls back everything
    }
}

// CORRECT: chunked, per-chunk transactions, skip-and-log, checkpoint
@Scheduled(cron = "0 0 2 * * *")
public void resilientJob() {
    String cursor = checkpointService.loadCheckpoint("interest");
    Page<Account> page;
    do {
        page = chunkProcessor.processChunk(cursor); // own TX
        if (!page.isEmpty()) {
            cursor = page.getContent().get(page.getContent().size() - 1).getId();
            checkpointService.saveCheckpoint("interest", cursor);
        }
    } while (!page.isEmpty());
}
```

```text
Naive approach risks:
1. OutOfMemoryError on large datasets
2. Hours-long transaction holding locks
3. Single failure rolls back all progress
4. No resumability after crash

Correct approach:
1. Flat memory via flush+clear
2. Per-chunk commits bound lock duration
3. Skip-and-log keeps the job running
4. Checkpoint enables crash recovery
```

### What should a senior engineer know about large scheduled jobs?

A senior engineer knows that the `@Scheduled` method itself should delegate to a service quickly and not hold the scheduler thread doing heavy work inline. They configure idempotent updates so re-running a chunk never double-counts. They monitor the job with metrics and alerts on both missed runs and duplicate runs. They run heavy jobs against a read replica where possible and schedule off-peak. They use Spring Batch when the dataset is large enough to warrant restartability, skip/retry, and audit.

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.Timer;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class MonitoredInterestService {

    private final Timer chunkTimer;

    public MonitoredInterestService(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.chunkTimer = Timer.builder("interest.chunk.duration")
                .description("Time per interest chunk")
                .register(registry);
    }

    public void processChunk(String cursor) {
        chunkTimer.record(() -> {
            // process one chunk
        });
    }
}
```

```text
Metrics to monitor:
- interest.chunk.duration: time per chunk (detect slowdowns)
- interest.job.total.rows: total rows processed
- interest.job.skipped: poison records skipped
- interest.job.status: success/failure per run
```
